# 3. AI in Software Development Life Cycle

## 3.1 Why AI in SDLC?

- **~55%** faster code generation with AI pair programming
- **~30%** reduction in testing effort using AI-based test generation tools
- **~3x** faster bug detection when AI is applied

Developers who learn to leverage AI in SDLC will be **10x** more productive than those who don't.

---

## 3.2 Phase 1 — Requirement Gathering & Analysis

This phase helps us understand **what to build and why** — gathering requirements, sorting them into *functional* (what the app does) and *non-functional* (how well it performs) needs.

Everything is captured in an **SRS (Software Requirements Specification)** document that guides the entire build.

### Use Case: Building an E-Commerce App

<details>
<summary><strong>Traditional Approach</strong></summary>

- Client shares the idea — *"I want to sell products online"*
- Product Owner asks targeted questions to understand the idea deeply — *What products? How will payments work?*
- Every answer is manually written down and organized into an SRS document
- Document is reviewed, corrected, and finalized

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>AI-Enhanced Approach</strong></summary>

- Paste the client's rough idea into an AI tool
- AI instantly asks the right questions and structures the requirements
- A categorized SRS-ready list is generated in minutes — covering features, performance needs, and edge cases

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Prompt</strong></summary>

>```
>I want to build an e-commerce app. List all requirements for it — group them into user features, admin features, and performance needs.
>```

</details>

**Tools:** Claude, ChatGPT

---

## 3.3 Phase 2 — Planning

Every great piece of software starts with a great plan.

This is where the project gets its direction — defining scope, timeline, budget, team, and identifying risks before a single line of code is written.

### Use Case: Building an E-Commerce App — Project Planning & Task Breakdown

<details>
<summary><strong>Traditional Approach</strong></summary>

- The SRS document from the previous phase is taken as a reference
- Each feature is picked one by one and time is estimated based on past experience
- Everything is manually organized into a final plan — takes a lot of time

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>AI-Enhanced Approach</strong></summary>

AI breaks features into smaller tasks, estimates time for each, and creates a realistic project plan in seconds.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Prompt</strong></summary>

>```
>I'm building an e-commerce app with these features: signup, login, product listing, search, cart, and payment. Break each feature into smaller tasks with time estimates. Give a simple timeline for 3 months.
>```

</details>

**Tools:** Claude, ChatGPT, Linear, Jira AI

---

## 3.4 Phase 3 — Design

This is the stage where requirements are transformed into **technical blueprints**, **prototypes**, and **system architectures** before coding begins.

### Use Case: Building an E-Commerce App — UI Layout Design

<details>
<summary><strong>Traditional Approach</strong></summary>

- Designers manually create screens and database structure
- Even a small change means reworking everything from scratch

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>AI-Enhanced Approach</strong></summary>

Describe what you want in plain text — get a working UI layout in seconds.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Prompt</strong></summary>

>```
>Generate a design template for an e-commerce product listing page. Use a modern minimal layout with a top navigation bar, left sidebar for category filters, and a grid layout for product cards showing image, name, price, and star rating. Use a clean white background with blue accent colors.
>```

</details>

**Tools:** Figma AI, Claude, Lovable

---

## 3.5 Phase 4 — Development

This is the stage where the actual **coding begins** — developers take the design blueprint and convert it into a working application, feature by feature.

### Use Case: Building an E-Commerce App — Cart Function

<details>
<summary><strong>Traditional Approach</strong></summary>

Developers write every line of code manually. Even a simple feature like cart calculation takes significant effort, and one small mistake anywhere can break the entire functionality.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>AI-Enhanced Approach</strong></summary>

AI-assisted coding (e.g., Copilot) increases speed and consistency. Just tell the AI what the cart should do in plain English — it writes the complete working code for you in seconds. The developer just reads it, checks it, and uses it.

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Prompt</strong></summary>

>```
>Write a function that takes a list of cart items where each item has a name, price, and quantity. Calculate the total price. If the total is above 1000, apply a 10% discount. If the total is below 500, add a delivery fee of 50 rupees.
>```

</details>

**Tools:** Claude Code, GitHub Copilot, Cursor

---

## 3.6 Phase 5 — Software Testing

In this phase, software testers perform various tests to validate the software and identify defects and bugs based on the requirements document.

### Use Case: Building an E-Commerce App — Testing the Cart Calculation

<details>
<summary><strong>Traditional Approach</strong></summary>

- Test cases are written manually for each feature
- Covering all scenarios takes significant effort
- Edge cases are often missed, leading to bugs reaching the live application

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>AI-Enhanced Approach</strong></summary>

- Paste the code into an AI tool
- AI automatically generates all possible test cases including edge cases
- Bugs are detected early before the application goes live

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>Prompt</strong></summary>

>```
>Here is a cart function that calculates the total price. Generate all possible test cases.
>```

</details>

**Tools:** Claude, Testim, Qodo AI

### Examples of Software Bugs/Defects

<details>
<summary><strong>Bug in the Pricing Functionality</strong></summary>

Customer added two items in the cart, but was charged for three.

- **Expected price:** 200 + 200 = 400
- **Actual price shown:** 600

This shows an example of a bug in the pricing functionality.

</details>

---

## 3.7 Phase 6 — Deployment

In this phase, the final product is delivered to end-users by making it available for download, access, or install.

<details>
<summary><strong>Traditional Approach</strong></summary>

In the Deployment phase, the following things should be considered:

- Physical environment (Setting up Servers)
- Documentation (User manuals, Installation Guides, Release Notes)
- Setting up links (Links or URLs for applications)
- Setting up a production database for users

*and many more...*

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>AI-Enhanced Approach</strong></summary>

Connect code to a deployment platform so that every update is automatically tested and goes live. If errors spike, it rolls back on its own in seconds.

</details>

**Tools:** Vercel, Netlify, Render, GitHub

---

## 3.8 Phase 7 — Maintenance

Software is never truly finished. It gets maintained.

This is the phase where software is released to the users, and any issues/defects that arise are addressed and fixed by the development team.

<details>
<summary><strong>Traditional Approach</strong></summary>

In the Maintenance phase, the following things should be considered:

- Gather feedback from users to identify areas of improvement in the application
- Maintain the software by fixing bugs and updating to ensure smooth operation
- Continuously improve the application by adding new features

*and many more...*

</details>

<details>
<summary><strong>AI-Enhanced Approach</strong></summary>

AI quickly reads all reviews and summarizes feature requests, bugs, and user feedback in seconds.

</details>

**Tools:** Claude, Sentry, Dependabot

---

## 3.9 Summary — SDLC Phases

| Phase | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| **Requirement Gathering & Analysis** | Understand what to build and why |
| **Planning** | Define scope, timeline, budget, and risks |
| **Design** | Create blueprints, prototypes, and system architectures |
| **Development** | Write code and build the application |
| **Software Testing** | Validate software and identify defects |
| **Deployment** | Deliver the product to end-users |
| **Maintenance** | Fix issues, add features, keep software running |

